# Taxation in India: From Budget to GST

> Taxes pay for everything the state does — and how a country taxes
> shapes its economy as much as how much it collects. This lesson
> maps India's tax system: direct vs indirect, the constitutional
> architecture, the century-long journey to **GST**, and the
> economics of who ultimately bears the burden.

## 1. Why Governments Tax

[definition]
Taxation serves three functions: **revenue** (funding public goods —
defence, roads, schools), **redistribution** (narrowing inequality
via progressive taxation), and **repricing** (discouraging
externalities — e.g., high excise on tobacco, coal cess).
[/definition]

Canonically: **Taxes are compulsory, unrequited payments** — you
don't get a specific good in return; you get the state.

## 2. The Two Great Families

| | **Direct taxes** | **Indirect taxes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| On whom levied | Income/wealth of persons & firms | Goods and services, transactions |
| Who pays the government | The person on whom it falls | The seller, who passes it on |
| Incidence (burden) | Cannot be shifted | Shifted to consumers via prices |
| Progressivity | Progressive (rich pay higher %) | Regressive (same rate for all incomes) |
| Examples | Income tax, corporate tax, capital gains, STT | GST, customs duty, excise |

**Buoyancy and elasticity:** direct taxes grow with income; indirect
taxes collect from everyone including the informal economy. India's
gross tax revenue divides roughly: ~one-fifth corporate tax,
~one-sixth income tax (direct together ~55–60%), GST ~one-fifth,
with the rest from union excise and customs.

## 3. The Constitutional Architecture

- **Article 265:** *No tax without authority of law.*
- **Seventh Schedule**: **Union List** (income tax beyond
  agricultural income, customs, corporate tax), **State List**
  (agricultural income tax, stamp duty, excise on liquor, vehicle
  tax), **Concurrent List** — taxes not expressly assigned stayed
  with the Union ( residuary).
- **Finance Commission (Article 280)** divides the divisible pool of
  central taxes between the Union and the states — the ballast of
  fiscal federalism.
- **Articles 268–281** enumerate the cesses, surcharges, and grants
  through which money flows.

[info title="Article 246A and the 101st Amendment"]
The **Constitution (101st Amendment) Act, 2016** inserted Articles
246A (simultaneous GST powers for Union and states), 269A (inter-
state GST), and 279A (the GST Council) — the only tax with both
Centre and state legislating on the same base.
[/info]

## 4. Direct Taxes Today

- **Personal income tax:** progressive slabs (new regime from FY
  2023–24: nil below ₹3 lakh, then 5–30% steps, with rebate for
  low incomes; surcharge above ₹50 lakh; marginal relief smooths
  the jump). First ₹1 lakh of income was taxed at only 8.5% back in
  1974 — rates once touched **97.75%** (1973–74); reforms since
  1991 cut both rates and slabs.
- **Corporate tax:** standard 22% (+ surcharge/cess) for domestic
  companies under the 2019 concessional regime; 15% for new
  manufacturing firms — a deliberate investment signal.
- **Capital gains,** STT, securities and wealth-adjacent levies
  round out the toolkit.
- **CBDT** administers direct taxes under the **Income Tax Act,
  1961**.

## 5. The Road to GST

Before July 2017, taxes cascaded: excise at the factory, entry tax
at each border, VAT (post-1999 state-level reform) on sales, service
tax (1994) on services — each levied on a price that already
contained the earlier tax ("tax on tax").

@chart line title="Stylised pre-GST cascade vs post-GST value-add tax on a ₹100 good"
labels:
Factory
Wholesaler
Retailer

series:
  - name: "Pre-GST (cumulative)"
    data: 10 18.5 27.6
  - name: "GST (on value added)"
    data: 10 18 26
@/chart

**GST (1 July 2017)** subsumed excise, service tax, VAT, entry tax,
octroi, and a dozen more into:

| Component | Within state | Across states |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Central GST (CGST) | ✓ | — |
| State GST (SGST)/UTGST | ✓ | — |
| Integrated GST (IGST) | — | ✓ (Centre collects, devolves) |

Four-rate structure **5 / 12 / 18 / 28%** (plus cess on luxury and
sin goods), a **compensation cess** guaranteeing states 14% annual
growth for five years, and the **GST Council (Article 279A)** —
Union finance minister + state finance ministers, votes weighted
1/3 Centre and 2/3 states, decisions by 3/4 majority.

[important]
**Why GST is called a game-changer:**
(1) **Input tax credit** kills the cascade — tax only on value
added at each stage;
(2) **One nation, one market** — no border checkpoints;
(3) **Formalisation** — credit chains pull traders into invoicing
and filing;
(4) revenue **transparency** — the invoice trail reconciles itself
across the chain.
[/important]

## 6. Critiques and Design Questions

- **Regressivity:** a uniform GST rate takes a bigger share of a
  poor household's budget; mitigations — exemptions/low slab for
  food, health, education.
- **Exclusion of real estate, electricity, petroleum products, and
  alcohol** keeps input-credit chains incomplete (petroleum enters
  GST for input credit only, 2022+).
- **Compliance burden** on small firms — composition scheme (1–6%
  flat, no credit) eases the smallest.
- **Rate rationalisation** debates continue — the July 2025
  restructure moved toward two main slabs (5% and 18%) with a 40%
  demerit band.

## 7. Indirect Taxes Beyond GST and the Black Economy

- **Customs duty** (tariffs) — trade policy instrument as much as
  revenue; **SAFTA/FTA** concessions.
- **Union excise** survives on **petroleum, tobacco** (not in GST).
- **Cesses and surcharges** (health cess, road cess) stay outside
  the divisible pool — a federal friction point.
- **Black money:** unaccounted income hidden from tax — attacked via
  demonetisation (2016), mandatory PAN/high-value transaction
  reporting, the **Black Money Act 2015**, faceless assessment, and
  the 2025 **Income Tax Act** rewrite aimed at simplification.

[summary]
India taxes along two axes: **direct** (income, corporate —
progressive, administered by CBDT, with today's peak 30% personal
marginal rate and 22% corporate) and **indirect** (GST, customs —
broad-based, regressive, now dominated by the GST's 5/12/18/28
architecture). The 101st Amendment's dual model (CGST/SGST/IGST)
with the GST Council replaced a cascading jungle with a value-added
system whose input credits tax only value addition. The system
keeps evolving — rate rationalisation, petroleum exclusion,
cess-versus-devolution federalism — under the constitutional rule
of Article 265: no tax without law.
[/summary]
